Original Wine 11 macdrv adapter for the publicly declared Apple host contract.

Interface facts are documented in Apple's public game-porting-toolkit formula,
revision 2bc44284e24d39ed64d6f492a0e1f4c47a5ced08 (macdrv_functions declarations).
No Apple or CrossOver implementation is copied. This original implementation
is offered under Wine's LGPL-2.1-or-later terms.

Build the PE driver and native driver together. Their private init transport is
extended; mixing either half with an older driver is unsupported. WoW64 transport
is updated as well; the Apple host table itself is exported only on x86_64.
Apply after wine-11.0-winemac-metal-exports.patch and
wine-11.0-dxmt-client-surface.patch. Existing DXMT opaque exports are unchanged.

The 14 explicitly Microsoft-ABI functions dispatch synchronously through Wine's
KeUserDispatchCallback into real PE ADVAPI32/USER32 operations. Return values,
ANSI/registry semantics and LastError are supplied by those APIs. The other ten
slots use native ABI and real upstream managed Wine windows, views and devices.
The historical 120-byte window ABI is an independently allocated snapshot, never
Wine 11's differently laid out private structure.

Monitor enumeration uses a typed PE callback relay, then an appended owned
Unix-call entry to invoke the native Microsoft-ABI callback through the
standard Wine Unix-call boundary. This retains native stack/TSD semantics,
original lparam, early-stop BOOL and LastError, without GS patches. Existing
Unix indices stay unchanged; the WoW64 table receives the same new entry.

Snapshot and Metal-view ownership are separate references. A process-local map
accepts only our managed client view and our returned Metal view. Unknown/stale
views return NULL; release never disposes of foreign objects. No map lock spans
Wine locks, PE callbacks or synchronous Cocoa work. A snapshot retains its actual
Cocoa window and matching current client view under Wine's lock solely
to acquire lifetime references; all Cocoa
view operations happen after unlocking. View release clears its surface pointer and releases only the owned Metal
view after in-flight layer calls finish; a still-owned snapshot can create
a replacement. Final context release delegates the surface to Wine's destructor. Window destruction
uses Wine's normal surface detach path; no fake window/view objects are created.

This source adapter has not established Apple D3DMetal game compatibility.

